  "tour": {
    "title": "Deuteronomy 22:26: No Sin Worthy of Death",
    "intro": "This short tour stays close to Deuteronomy 22:23–27 and the phrase “no sin worthy of death.” It first gives you the legal and social setting, then walks through the field scenario itself, and finally steps back to consider how these laws function within Deuteronomy’s vision of life together.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "naked-bible:podcast:2582",
        "startSec": 2338,
        "endSec": 2428,
        "why": "Start here for the setting behind the passage. The teacher explains that a betrothed woman had the legal status of “neighbor’s wife,” and contrasts the city case with the open-country case, where “there was no one to rescue her.” That makes clear why verse 26 is not excusing the assault but distinguishing the woman’s guilt from the man’s violence.",
        "episodeTitle": "Naked Bible 289: Exodus 20 Part 3",
        "sourceId": "naked-bible",
        "source": "Naked Bible Podcast",
        "audioUrl": "https://nakedbiblepodcast.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/NakedBible289_Exodus20_Part3.mp3",
        "durationSec": 4791,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "forty-minutes-ot:podcast:30fde043e9e643b3bb4d8c55275aa197",
        "startSec": 782,
        "endSec": 870,
        "why": "This is the most direct treatment of the verse. The speakers identify the woman in the field as innocent because she cries out without help, and say the text “equates rape with murder in a sense” by comparing the assault to a man rising against and murdering his neighbor. It also briefly contrasts this with a consensual sexual case at the end of the chapter.",
        "episodeTitle": "Episode 175 (Deuteronomy 22:13-24:4)",
        "sourceId": "forty-minutes-ot",
        "source": "40 Minutes in the Old Testament",
        "audioUrl": "https://adbarker.com/stream/tfVE3XBxC9JCmb97VsZt7vpT/traffic.libsyn.com/secure/40minot/40_Min_ep_175.mp3?dest-id=245197",
        "durationSec": 2424,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "spoken-gospel:podcast:d362f57c-978c-4849-b13f-16e08c02b608",
        "startSec": 250,
        "endSec": 430,
        "why": "End with the wider interpretive frame. The speakers suggest that these laws unpack “don’t murder” into preserving life in the community, including laws about rape and deceptive or consensual relationships; they also discuss the alternative that the varied laws show God’s rule touching “every facet of life.” This helps you hear verse 26 as part of Deuteronomy’s concern for communal justice, not as an isolated technical phrase.",
        "episodeTitle": "Deuteronomy 22-25: Miscellaneous Laws",
        "sourceId": "spoken-gospel",
        "source": "Spoken Gospel",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/SPG1734965801.mp3?updated=1664486113",
        "durationSec": 3744,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      }
    ],
    "closing": "The central distinction these clips draw is sharp: Deuteronomy says the assaulted woman has “no sin worthy of death” because the circumstances testify that she was resisting and could not be rescued, while the man’s act is compared to murder. The passage is difficult, but its immediate movement is toward protecting the victim from being treated as the guilty party.",
